{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/278040-brythonic-pantheon-wip","id":278040,"title":"Brythonic Pantheon (WIP)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-10-11T03:06:16-07:00","id":278040,"title":"Brythonic Pantheon (WIP)","content":"Brythonic Pantheon<br /> I\u2019ve been trying to come up with a fair and useable British pantheon for \u2018modern\u2019 scion purposes. I\u2019ll post what I have so far: if anyone has any ideas for amendments or to fill in the gaps, please let me know and I\u2019ll add in your ideas.<br /> The list I have come up with incorporates deities that have been worshipped since Roman times, up to the present day. I don\u2019t want to include those deities that can be seen as aspects of existing Scion Gods/Goddesses, particularly the Dodekatheon, so derivatives of Roman Gods such as Mars are out as they tie in with Ares. So far, the Gods I have listed are based on Wiccan, Roman, Norman and to a certain extent Celtic deities. Saxon Gods are out as they are derivatives of the Aesir.<br /> I have deliberately omitted semi-mythical characters such as Boudica, King Arthur and Robin Hood as they were never worshipped as Gods and (for gaming purposes) are more likely to have been Scions themselves.<br /> So far the Pantheon look like this;<br /> <b>Ankou</b>; God of Death, the Grave Guardian.<br /> Animal (Owl), Civitas, Death, Epic Appearance, Guardian, Prophecy, Psychopomp<br /> Control, ?<br /> <b>Andraste</b>; Goddess of war and victory.<br /> Animal (Hare), Civitas, Epic Charisma, Epic Strength, Epic Stamina, War<br /> Command, Control, Fortitude, Integrity, Melee, Politics<br /> <b>Aradia</b>; the Mother Goddess, the Moon Goddess, the Triple Goddess, chief of the Pantheon.<br /> Civitas, Earth, Epic Intelligence, Epic Stamina, Epic Strength, Fertility, Guardian, Magic, Moon<br /> Academics, Art, Athletics, Craft, Fortitude, Presence<br /> <b>Britannia</b>; the Avatar of Britain, Goddess of valour, leadership, and the sea.<br /> Animal (Lion), Civitas, Epic Charisma, Epic Intelligence, Guardian, Justice, Water<br /> Academics, Awareness, Command, Integrity, Politics, Presence<br /> <b>Cernunnos</b>; the Horned God, the Green Man, Lord of the Hunt. Consort of Aradia.<br /> Animal (Stag, Dog), Civitas, Death, Epic Appearance, Epic Perception, Epic Stamina, Epic Strength, Fertility, Psychopomp, Sun<br /> Animal Ken, Athletics, Fortitude, Marksmanship, Presence, Survival<br /> <b>Sulis</b>; Goddess of health, springs, curses and blessings.<br /> Animal (Snake), Civitas, Guardian, Health, Magic, Water<br /> Medicine, ?<br /> Thoughts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4057-darkusmarkus","name":"DarkusMarkus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-11T12:36:26-07:00","id":278305,"title":"","content":"Hmmm<br /> Not a bad list...Although I don't think I'd add Cernunnos to it. (Or perhaps I wouldn't use the name.)<br /> <br /> I think I'd use <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nodens\" target=\"_blank\">Nodens</a> instead who seems to be very British;<br /> <i><br /> is a Celtic deity associated with healing, the sea, hunting and dogs. He was worshipped in ancient Britain, most notably in a temple complex at Lydney Park in Gloucestershire, and possibly also in Gaul. He is equated with the Roman gods Mars, Mercury, Neptune and Silvanus, and his name is cognate with that of the Irish mythological figure Nuada and the Welsh Nudd.</i><br /> <br /> I have to say trying to pigeonhole the deities floating around the region into a purely &quot;Brythonic&quot; pantheon is an interesting challenge in light of all the Roman occupation and how they interpret the gods and Gaelic deities imported that also became a part of the whole mish-mash. Heck, you could add Welsh deities and still be a bit accurate considering a lot of old kings were Welsh. For example, Gwyn ap Nudd who is something of a vicious bastard of war (and torture) is also a psychopomp (emphasis on the word &quot;psycho&quot; perhaps.) that collects the souls of dead British warriors. Bran the Blessed, a giant figure of Welsh legend and a former king of Britain, demanded to be buried in the hill facing France as a ward against invasion. the list can go on, but it pretty much ends with the later tales of Arthur.<br /> <br /> No Brigid?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-12T08:30:18-07:00","id":278826,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the feedback!<br /> Unfortunately Brigid is already in the Irish pantheon; the Tuatha. Cernunnos has a few different names, one of which I could use instead (Herne is a viable alternative), but if anything he's the quintessential member of the pantheon along with Aradia: Together they form the duality which the Wiccan faith revolves around. Plus he's kinda my favourite, so I don't want him to go!<br /> I considered Nodens, but as he is equated with a Roman God I had to discount him; he's too close to Ares (otherwise known as Mars in the Scion world). Welsh heroes such as Gwyn are an option, but the key word is 'hero'. Like Arthur, they're not Gods, they're mortals and more likely considered to be ancient Scions, only a few of which were elevated to Godhood.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4057-darkusmarkus","name":"DarkusMarkus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-12T15:05:59-07:00","id":279107,"title":"","content":"*shrugs*<br /> I think you have your heart set on certain ideas that don't seem accurate from a historical perspective...but it's me, so it's all good.<br /> Cernunnos served a wide area and is typically considered Gaelic (i.e. that is Goidelic, as opposed to Brythonic...and where is the &quot;ram-headed snake&quot; he's supposed to be associated with?)<br /> Brigid (i.e. &quot;the arrow of fire&quot;) also served a wide area but I would think juxtapose Sulis' water element nicely. (The fire element seems to be missing in your list)<br /> Most of the deities known in that region are only known through Roman sources. (Which I admit to a degree of frustration. But whenever I tried to get away from Roman sources, I only found ancient Welsh, the people which seemed to typically rule Britain anyway.)<br /> <br /> Curious, are you Wiccan? (I wrote a rather large post on the subject, which I still find very interesting)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-13T06:44:38-07:00","id":279595,"title":"","content":"No, but I have friends who are and I did a lot of background reading. So what doesn't seem historically accurate? I admit I got most of my info from Wikipedia so it may not be entirely spot on lol. Cernunnos seems to appear in several forms in various different religions. The ram-headed snake could be added in but seeing as he has two animal forms already I thought it a bit much. Could be a new Creature to add to the Birthrights list...In any case, for gaming purposes the pantheon can have a bit of flexibility imo.<br /> I would consider Brigid a different entity altogether than Sulis, although I do see the similarities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4057-darkusmarkus","name":"DarkusMarkus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-13T10:04:34-07:00","id":279804,"title":"","content":"I wouldn't be trying to go for historical &quot;accuracy&quot; in this project, insofar for the values of what you seem to be trying to accomplish. A &quot;Brythonic&quot; pantheon is going to be VERY hard to reconstruct. It's going to have to be like the Gaulish pantheon, based on a lot of speculation on a very little evidence most of which is coming from outsiders who are heavy into syncretism (e.g. the Romans) and a lot of comparative mythology that can go off in very odd directions. <br /> <br /> For values of &quot;accuracy&quot;, the Welsh &quot;pantheon is probably&quot; the closest thing we'll ever get to a &quot;Brythonic&quot; pantheon and that is through the lens of ex-pagan reductionism, turning gods into heroes, etc. But the Welsh are pretty much the direct descendants of what was left of the pre-Roman Celtic civilization of the southern British Isles. (Barring, of course, the very persistent underlying Celtic culture throughout the duration of Roman dominance, e.g. the Men of the North material, which was preserved in Welsh tradition in any case...).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}